BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261002T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261002T120000Z
DTEND:20261002T130000Z
SUMMARY:On the Coalgebraic Linear-Time Semantics of Two-Player Games (Ben P
 lummer (University of Birmingham))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Ben Plummer (University of Birmingham)\nTheoretical Computer Sc
 ience Seminar\n\nLinear-time semantics play an important role in program s
 emantics and verification\, in settings where branching-time equivalence i
 s too fine. Set-based coalgebra provides an account of branching-time beha
 viour\, while finite linear-time semantics can be treated coalgebraically 
 by moving to the Kleisli category of a monad modelling the system’s bran
 ching. In this talk\, I will discuss how these ideas extend to the linear-
 time semantics of two-player games. For finite behaviour\, the coalgebraic
  approach applies smoothly to games in which the opponent’s choices are 
 nondeterministic\, probabilistic\, or weighted. We characterise the result
 ing semantics in terms of strategies that enforce…\n\nDetails: https://r
 esearchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/84/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/84/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261023T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261023T120000Z
DTEND:20261023T130000Z
SUMMARY:Semantics and intermediate representations of probabilistic program
 s (Hugo Paquet (INRIA Paris))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Hugo Paquet (INRIA Paris)\nTheoretical Computer Science Seminar
 \n\nIn a probabilistic programming language (with only finite types and wi
 thout recursion or loops) every program determines a probability distribut
 ion over a set of outputs. Computing this distribution can be expensive: t
 he naive approach is to enumerate all possible executions. So practical in
 terpreters rely on more efficient representations of probability distribut
 ions\, typically inspired by symbolic execution. In this talk I will give 
 a semantic perspective on these intermediate representations\, to explain 
 why they are correct. The key idea is to decompose probabilistic sampling 
 into two separate effects (first generating a random variable\, and then r
 eading its value) that follow different…\n\nDetails: https://researchsem
 inars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/85/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/85/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261030T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261030T130000Z
DTEND:20261030T140000Z
SUMMARY:Handlers of Parametrised Algebraic Theories (Jack Liell-Cock (Unive
 rsity of Oxford))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Jack Liell-Cock (University of Oxford)\nTheoretical Computer Sc
 ience Seminar\n\nThe algebraic effects and handlers paradigm separates the
  concerns of the interface and implementation of computational effects in 
 programming languages. We present Cambria\, a language that extends this f
 ramework to the parametrised setting. Effect signatures may use abstract p
 arameter types that are instantiated by the handler along with the operati
 on implementations. Parameters abstract over resources\, such as memory lo
 cations or thread IDs\, permitting algebraic effects to encode dynamic all
 ocation. They are first-class in the type system but erased at runtime\, r
 equiring no coercions or type-directed reduction. We prove parametricity v
 ia a step-indexed logical relation\, formalizing the…\n\nDetails: https:
 //researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/86/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/86/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261106T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261106T130000Z
DTEND:20261106T140000Z
SUMMARY:My thoughts on AI and security (Mark Ryan (University of Birmingham
 ))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Mark Ryan (University of Birmingham)\nTheoretical Computer Scie
 nce Seminar\n\nThe first part of my talk will be about "prompt injection"\
 , in which an adversary inserts text in content (such as a paper\, a githu
 b README\, a CV or an email message) that will be processed by an LLM\, in
  an effort to instruct the LLM to process the content the way the adversar
 y wants. Large language models read instructions and data as one stream of
  tokens\, so anyone who can get text into that stream can potentially have
  it obeyed as a command. I'll review real attacks\, and then compare three
  kinds of defence\, focussing on the type that seems to have the greatest 
 capability to work. In a possible second part\, I'll describe a bit about 
 how I use AI in practice\, what has worked and what has…\n\nDetails: htt
 ps://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/87/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/87/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261113T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261113T130000Z
DTEND:20261113T140000Z
SUMMARY:Code in the Age of Agents: Bottlenecks\, Languages\, and the New So
 ftware Reality (Dan Ghica (University of Birmingham))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Dan Ghica (University of Birmingham)\nTheoretical Computer Scie
 nce Seminar\n\nEverything has changed\, and the software engineering lands
 cape will never look the same. Autonomous coding agents can already produc
 e vast implementations\, dismantling the long-held assumption that writing
  code is the primary constraint of software development. As the marginal c
 ost of code generation becomes negligible\, where does the real bottleneck
  relocate? Is the new point of friction in comprehension\, review\, and ve
 rification\, or does the critical challenge move even higher up the stack\
 , in specification and validation? Furthermore\, agent-generated code diff
 ers fundamentally from human-written systems in style\, cognitive idioms\,
  and architectural failure modes (e.g. slop: plausible…\n\nDetails: http
 s://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/88/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/88/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261120T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261120T130000Z
DTEND:20261120T140000Z
SUMMARY:Space-Efficient Hierholzer (Ziad Ismaili  Alaoui (University of Liv
 erpool))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Ziad Ismaili  Alaoui (University of Liverpool)\nTheoretical Com
 puter Science Seminar\n\nHere is a popular challenge: Can you copy a given
  drawing without lifting your pen or tracing any line more than once? A fa
 miliar example is the "Haus vom Nikolaus" (house of Santa Claus): a square
  with both diagonals and a triangular roof. In graph-theoretic terms\, thi
 s is the same as asking for an Eulerian trail\; that is\, a walk that trav
 erses every edge exactly once. (In this talk\, we focus on Eulerian cycles
 \, where the endpoints of the trail are equal.) The question of finding Eu
 lerian cycles or characterising which graphs have one goes back to Euler's
  work on the bridges of Königsberg\, which many cite as the birth of grap
 h theory. Hierholzer's algorithm is a classical method for…\n\nDetails: 
 https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/89/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/89/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261127T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261127T130000Z
DTEND:20261127T140000Z
SUMMARY:The finitary aspects of Full Ground Local State (Orpheas van Rooij 
 (University of Edinburgh))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Orpheas van Rooij (University of Edinburgh)\nTheoretical Comput
 er Science Seminar\n\nFull ground local state (FGLS) refers to dynamically
  allocated mutable state that allows storing ground values and references.
  It is a key ingredient in many imperative algorithms as it enables cyclic
  data structures. In this work\, we focus on one particular denotational m
 odel for FGLS: Kammar et al.'s possible worlds monad on sets indexed over 
 sets of locations. We resolve an outstanding question about this FGLS mona
 d: is it finitary? We show that it is not\, by showing the existence of no
 n-finitary computations in this monad that are challenging to define in a 
 terminating language with full ground references. This prompts us to intro
 duce a finitary submonad of Kammar et al.'s monad\, which…\n\nDetails: h
 ttps://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/90/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/90/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tcs-20261204T1300@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261204T130000Z
DTEND:20261204T140000Z
SUMMARY:Algebraically free Markov chains (Paolo Perrone (University of Oxfo
 rd))
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Paolo Perrone (University of Oxford)\nTheoretical Computer Scie
 nce Seminar\n\nAlgebraically free monoids\, and more generally monads\, ar
 e commonly used to model operations that can be 'folded'\, such as repeate
 d actions of a set of 'elementary moves'. Notable examples are the writer 
 monad (free over the alphabet set)\, and the free monad over a graph. In c
 ategory theory\, an algebraically free monoid over an object X is a monoid
  FX together with a bijection between X-algebras and FX-algebras\, with th
 e intuition that FX-algebras are "just X-algebras where the action is iter
 ated". In the theory of dynamical systems and Markov chains\, algebras can
  be decategorified to invariant states or invariant measures. Similarly\, 
 we can decategorify a monoid object to an idempotent…\n\nDetails: https:
 //researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/91/
CATEGORIES:Theoretical Computer Science Seminar
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://researchseminars.org/talk/TheoryCSBham/91/
END:VEVENT
