UID:iop-20261006T1930@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261006T183000Z
DTEND:20261006T193000Z
SUMMARY:Mysteries of matter: new experiments to unpick the nature of dark m
 atter (Dr Daniel Johnson)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Dr Daniel Johnson\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n
 \nContrary to its name\, there is nothing hidden about dark matter. It's t
 here in plain sight\, dramatically changing the way galaxies move and impr
 inted all over the incredible pictures astronomers take of the cosmos. But
 \, whilst we know for sure that it is there\, we have no idea what it's ma
 de of and that makes particle physicists deeply uncomfortable. This evenin
 g we will explore new methods to tease out truths about the particle ident
 ity of dark matter\, and how new experiments might soon shed light on this
  mysterious matter that\, overwhelmingly\, dominates the 'stuff' in the Un
 iverse.\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineer
 ing-and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liais
 on/outreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
CATEGORIES:Institute of Physics Public Lecture Series
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-
 sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-s
 econdary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iop-20261103T1930@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261103T193000Z
DTEND:20261103T203000Z
SUMMARY:The Invisible Scanners (Jim Grozier)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Jim Grozier\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n\nThis
  talk covers a period in the recent history of particle physics (approxima
 tely the 1960s and 1970s) when the output of particle colliders was in the
  form of thousands of photographs of bubble chamber events\, and large num
 bers of relatively unskilled women were brought in to examine and analyse 
 these photographs in search of patterns which might reveal evidence of new
  particles or unusual decays. These women were largely “invisible” in 
 that they were not named in research papers and received little reward\, d
 espite the fact that they were at the forefront of discovery. In this\, th
 eir story bears some comparison with those of the “Harvard Computers” 
 – women who analysed astronomical…\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.
 ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astro
 nomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-publi
 c-lecture-series
CATEGORIES:Institute of Physics Public Lecture Series
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-
 sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-s
 econdary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iop-20261201T1930@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261201T193000Z
DTEND:20261201T203000Z
SUMMARY:Is it a star? Is it a planet? No\, it's a brown dwarf! (Dr Jenni Fr
 ench)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Dr Jenni French\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n\n
 Brown dwarfs are the Universe’s awkward middle child\, not quite a star\
 , not quite a planet\, but they are fascinating in their own right. Acting
  as the intermediary objects between planets and stars\, brown dwarfs have
  complex atmospheres and act as natural laboratories for a plethora of exo
 planet science. This talk explores brown dwarfs\, what they are\, how we f
 ind them\, and why they’re so interesting\, as well as how you can get i
 nvolved in their discovery.\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about
 /college-of-engineering-and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outrea
 ch-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-public-lecture-s
 eries
CATEGORIES:Institute of Physics Public Lecture Series
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-
 sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-s
 econdary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
END:VEVENT
