UID:pp-20261118T1230@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261118T123000Z
DTEND:20261118T133000Z
SUMMARY:The Timepix4 Beam Telescope (David Bacher (University of Oxford (GB
 )))
LOCATION:Small Lecture Theatre (Poynting)
DESCRIPTION:David Bacher (University of Oxford (GB))\nParticle Physics Semi
 nars\n\nAt the High-Luminosity LHC\, LHCb Upgrade II will record about 25 
 proton–proton collisions per bunch crossing. At this pile-up\, assigning
  each heavy-flavour decay to its primary vertex requires full 4D tracking 
 in the vertex detector\, with a time resolution of about 20 ps per track. 
 No existing silicon pixel tracker reaches this\, so new sensors and readou
 t ASICs need years of development and a way to test them with real particl
 es. To test these prototypes\, the Timepix4 beam telescope at the CERN SPS
  measures each beam particle precisely in both space and time. Its eight T
 imepix4 planes predict where the particle crosses the prototype with 2.3 
 µm precision\, while a common clock system and a…\n\nDetails: https://i
 ndico.global/event/18930/
CATEGORIES:Particle Physics Seminars
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://indico.global/event/18930/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pp-20261202T1330@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261127T155446Z
DTSTART:20261202T133000Z
DTEND:20261202T143000Z
SUMMARY:Weak Boson Scattering at the Large Hadron Collider\, Circa 2030 and
  Beyond.
LOCATION:Small Lecture Theatre (Poynting)
DESCRIPTION:Particle Physics Seminars\n\nThe Large Hadron Collider (LHC) is
  a remarkable multipurpose accelerator complex. With a proton-proton cente
 r-of-mass energy of 14 TeV\, it allows one to study directly\, in a labora
 tory setting\, the scattering of elementary particles at energies well bey
 ond the electroweak scale. This include the scattering of two incoming W o
 r Z bosons\, a process known as "weak boson scattering" or more broadly as
  "vector boson scattering" (VBS). While long predicted\, these ultra rare 
 processes were observed for the first time only during Run II and III of t
 he LHC's operation\, and will be a physics driver of the high-luminosity (
 HL-LHC) program.In this talk\, I will give a broad overview of the status 
 and…\n\nDetails: https://indico.global/event/18932/
CATEGORIES:Particle Physics Seminars
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://indico.global/event/18932/
END:VEVENT
